# 1. Scope

This strategy governs verification of the authenticated avatar upload and crop capability for Issue AD-488 across the full request path from browser interaction through gateway admission, synchronous malware inspection, structural file validation, cloud persistence, CDN invalidation, and dynamic UI propagation. The scope is intentionally constrained to a single high-resolution avatar transaction and is centered on the architecture described in the approved business and technical handoff. The governing principle for this plan is that the browser is not a transformation engine. The front-end is permitted to present controls, collect the file, and transmit the raw binary, but all material image handling and trust decisions are performed on controlled backend components. This has direct testing implications: validation must not stop at user interface rendering or API response codes, but must prove the correct routing path, execution order, and infrastructure side effects across every backend control point.

A primary architectural concern in scope is access gating. The avatar modification interface must remain hidden or disabled whenever a fresh, unexpired authentication token is not present in the browser cookie session payload. This means the testing scope includes both presentation-layer gating and gateway-layer enforcement. It is not sufficient to verify that a button disappears for unauthenticated users; the strategy must also prove that forged, stale, missing, near-expiry, and replayed token conditions are rejected by the dedicated API Gateway using the expected 401 Unauthorized response contract and the standard unauthorized block message. The gateway itself is a scoped component because it is the first enforceable trust boundary for upload admission, request filtering, bandwidth protection, and downstream service isolation.

The second major architectural theme in scope is controlled ingress and server-side processing discipline. Upload transactions must traverse the dedicated API Gateway path and may not bypass it through alternate hosts, direct service addressing, or misconfigured backend routes. The gateway must enforce payload-size constraints, authentication state, and admission filtering before the request enters deeper processing layers. After gateway admission, the platform must synchronously perform inline malware inspection and deep file header parsing using structural file signature validation rather than extension trust. Because the implementation explicitly forbids client-side compression, scaling, or canvas manipulation, the strategy must also include evidence collection proving the browser transfers a raw binary payload without mutation and that any crop operation represented in the user flow is executed or recorded on the backend service side rather than through browser-resident image processing logic.

The final major scope element is downstream consistency after successful persistence. Validated avatar images are stored into specialized cloud bucket folders within the authorized cloud ecosystem only, and that write event must trigger immediate CDN edge invalidation so that profile surfaces and header bars display the updated avatar without full page reload. Testing therefore extends beyond storage success into cache coherency verification, stale-edge elimination, and asynchronous UI state refresh behavior. The 2.0-second SLA under standard 4G network conditions applies to the complete chain of upload transport, gateway handling, malware scan, persistence, invalidation, and public profile availability. For this reason, the scope includes instrumented timing collection at each checkpoint and cross-session visual propagation checks across multiple active user views.

## 1.1 In Scope
- Authenticated avatar upload flow
- Avatar crop capability as exposed in the user story and validated as server-side processing or backend-recorded crop behavior
- Front-end access gating for avatar modification interface
- Dedicated API Gateway routing and bypass-prevention controls for upload transactions
- Gateway authentication rejection and standard 401 Unauthorized block messaging
- Server-side image ingestion handling
- Raw binary transfer verification without client-side compression or transcoding
- Synchronous inline malware inspection
- Deep file header and magic-number validation
- 5MB upload size boundary enforcement, including exact 5.00MB pass and 5.01MB fail conditions
- JPEG and WebP whitelist enforcement
- Authorized cloud bucket storage verification limited to approved cloud ecosystem
- Immediate CDN cache invalidation after successful upload
- Dynamic UI synchronization across active views and header bars
- End-to-end SLA validation under 4G proxy restriction

## 1.2 Out of Scope
- Client-side compression tools
- Local image scaling engines
- Front-end canvas manipulation scripts as an implementation feature
- Processing of PNG files
- Processing of SVG files
- Processing of GIF files
- Processing of raw design files
- Concurrent batch uploads
- Multi-image profile header adjustments
- Storage infrastructures outside the authorized cloud ecosystem

# 3. Objectives

The objective of this strategy is to provide defensible, auditable verification that the avatar upload capability behaves safely, correctly, and within operational performance limits under realistic user and infrastructure conditions. The first objective is to prove access control integrity from the browser through the API Gateway. This includes validation that authenticated users with a fresh, unexpired session token can discover and use the avatar workflow, while users with missing, expired, near-expiry, malformed, or replayed cookies are blocked both visually and transactionally. The objective extends to demonstrating that unauthorized sessions receive the standardized 401 Unauthorized handling path, including gateway rejection and the expected UI block message contract, with no accidental exposure of upload controls or backend endpoints.

The second objective is to prove data integrity and execution ordering across the server-side ingestion chain. The front end must transmit the raw image binary without browser-side compression, transformation, or local rendering-based mutation. The dedicated gateway must be the only admissible route into the pipeline, and size-based enforcement for 5MB constraints must occur at the gateway boundary quickly enough to avoid wasteful downstream load. Once admitted, the system must perform malware scanning synchronously and complete deep file signature inspection before persistence. This objective requires timestamped evidence that malware scan completion and header validation precede storage writes and that disguised files renamed as JPEG or WebP are rejected based on structural bytes, not file extension strings.

The third objective is to prove post-persistence distribution correctness and freshness. A successful write must land in the designated specialized cloud bucket location within the authorized provider boundaries and trigger immediate CDN invalidation so that new content supersedes stale edge cache copies. The strategy must verify that profile pages, account menus, and header bars update through dynamic DOM refresh without full browser reload, and that multiple active sessions converge on the new avatar without delay inconsistent with the two-second end-to-end service objective. This objective also covers the prevention of cache desynchronization, ensuring that edge nodes do not continue serving previous assets after invalidation requests have been issued.

The fourth objective is to establish a release-management control framework that ties together requirements, defects, risk mitigation, automation, observability, and human approval checkpoints. The strategy is not merely to detect bugs, but to make release readiness measurable. It must produce traceability from each acceptance criterion to tests, ensure CI/CD gates prevent unsafe deployment, and support a controlled pause state for Human-in-the-Loop Gate 3 pending manual validation approval. The combined outcome is a technical specification and operating model that allows engineering, QA, security, and release stakeholders to determine whether AD-488 is fit for promotion with known residual risk explicitly documented.

# 4. Test Approach

## 4.1.1 Test Types
Describe all the test types in this project and provide the general testing timeline.

### Functional Testing
Functional testing will validate the user-visible and service-visible behavior of the avatar workflow across the Component/UI, API, Service/Integration, and System/E2E layers. Execution will begin in QA using seeded authenticated and unauthenticated session states, then expand into UAT and Pre-Prod once contract stability is confirmed. At the UI layer, Playwright will verify that avatar controls are hidden when no fresh cookie-based session exists and become enabled only when a valid token is present. At the API layer, PyTest-based service tests will call the gateway-backed upload endpoint with controlled token permutations, size-boundary files, and valid JPEG and WebP assets. Integration stages will validate ordered execution from gateway admission to malware scan to header validation to storage to CDN purge. Functional data includes clean JPEG and WebP files under the threshold, exactly 5.00MB files, and synchronized multi-session browser views. Evidence collection will include DOM snapshots, HAR files, gateway logs, storage events, purge notifications, and end-state asset URL verification to ensure behavior matches acceptance criteria without reliance on informal manual observation.

### Security Testing
Security testing will focus on enforcing trust boundaries around authentication, gateway routing, malware inspection, structural file validation, and storage confinement within the authorized cloud ecosystem. This testing will be performed in QA, a service-integration environment, and Pre-Prod, where OWASP ZAP and PyTest security harnesses can exercise both browser and API attack surfaces while preserving auditability. The execution model will include token tampering, expired cookie replay, near-expiry clock-skew conditions, direct-service bypass attempts, spoofed MIME headers, renamed script payloads, malformed binaries, and malware-positive samples approved for safe use. Clean-room sample sets will include benign text files renamed as .jpg, executable-like binaries with forged content types, truncated JPEGs, malformed WebP assets, and traffic directed at alternate backend hosts to prove gateway exclusivity. Security testing must also confirm that uploads rejected at the gateway do not produce scanner invocations, storage writes, or CDN events. ZAP will evaluate request tampering and authorization weaknesses, while log correlation will confirm that server-side controls are engaged in the correct order and that no policy bypass path exists under hostile inputs.

### Negative Testing
Negative testing will intentionally supply invalid, malformed, unsupported, mistimed, and policy-breaking inputs to verify that the platform fails safely and predictably. This suite will run continuously in QA and nightly in Pre-Prod using PyTest and Playwright, with integration observability enabled so that every rejection path can be correlated across gateway, scan, storage, and UI layers. Inputs will include missing authentication cookies, expired tokens, near-expiry tokens affected by clock skew, oversize payloads at 5.01MB and above, unsupported file categories, renamed text files, malformed header byte streams, malware-positive samples, interrupted uploads, and stale-client attempts to force cached avatar display after a successful replacement. Data requirements include deterministic invalid assets, traffic-shaping profiles, and session matrices across browsers. Negative testing will specifically prove that over-limit payloads are instantly dropped at the gateway rather than rejected downstream, that unauthorized sessions receive the standard 401 message contract, and that no partial persistence or cache invalidation occurs after a failed transaction. The emphasis is on strong failure isolation and prevention of hidden side effects.

### Boundary Testing
Boundary testing will validate that the system behaves correctly at the precise acceptance thresholds defined by the story, particularly the 5MB upload cap and the authentication freshness decision points. Execution will occur in QA and Pre-Prod with controlled data fabrication so file sizes can be generated just below 5.00MB, exactly at 5.00MB, and just above at 5.01MB or more. Session data will include valid, almost-expired, and expired tokens to explore authorization cutover boundaries under realistic clock-skew assumptions. PyTest will drive direct API assertions against gateway responses, while Playwright will confirm the corresponding interface messages and control states. Additional boundary sets will cover minimally valid JPEG and WebP files, large-dimension but compliant images, and binary streams whose first bytes nearly resemble valid magic numbers but diverge before full signature confirmation. Boundary evidence must show that threshold values are interpreted consistently across UI hints, gateway admission logic, backend validation services, and observability logs. Timing boundaries will also be measured by decomposing the two-second SLA into sub-budgets for transport, security inspection, persistence, invalidation, and UI refresh propagation.

### Integration Testing
Integration testing will validate the correctness of the end-to-end orchestration among the browser client, API Gateway, malware-scanning service, file-signature validator, authorized cloud storage bucket, CDN invalidation mechanism, and UI synchronization channel. This work will run in a service-integrated QA environment and then in Pre-Prod with production-like infrastructure policies. The execution pattern will use correlation identifiers propagated from the initial upload request through each downstream event so that sequence integrity can be proven. Test data will include clean files, infected samples, spoofed-header binaries, and exact-threshold files. The suite will verify that the front-end sends a raw binary, that the gateway is the only admissible entry point, that scan completion timestamps precede storage writes, that successful storage emits the expected bucket event, and that CDN purge requests reference the updated object path. Browser tests in multiple sessions will then confirm dynamic avatar refresh without full page reload. Integration results are only considered passing when logs, events, and user-facing outcomes all align and no out-of-order behavior or hidden side effect is detected.

### Performance Testing
Performance testing will measure whether the complete upload-to-distribution chain can satisfy the strict 2.0-second SLA under a standard 4G network-throttled profile. This testing will be executed primarily in Pre-Prod using JMeter for API throughput and latency collection, Playwright for real-browser timing, and tcconfig to impose deterministic network shaping representative of constrained mobile conditions. The dataset will include valid JPEG and WebP files close to the upper file-size boundary, because those inputs represent the highest realistic processing cost within scope. Measurements will segment total elapsed time into upload transport, gateway decision, malware scan duration, file-signature validation, storage commitment, CDN invalidation acknowledgment, and browser-visible avatar refresh. Additional runs will compare warm versus cold cache behavior to ensure the purge mechanism does not create hidden latency regressions. Performance testing also includes resilience checks for scanner timeout behavior and gateway back-pressure under moderate parallel demand, while respecting the functional scope of single avatar upload transactions per request. A pass requires both aggregate SLA compliance and absence of performance-induced correctness failures such as stale avatar display or duplicate writes.

