## 4.3 Test Management and Defect Management
Test execution will be governed in Jira using a standardized workflow aligned to engineering delivery and release reporting. All test stories, test cases, defects, automation tasks, environment issues, and release blockers will be traceable to Issue AD-488. The core defect workflow is Open -> Triaged -> In Progress -> Ready for QA -> Verified. New issues enter Open when raised by QA, automation, engineering, security, or release management. During Triaged, a daily defect council composed of QA lead, engineering lead, product owner, and where needed security engineering confirms reproducibility, severity, component ownership, rollback exposure, and release impact. Once accepted, the assignee moves the issue to In Progress and links the corrective pull request. After code completion and peer review, the defect transitions to Ready for QA only when the build is deployed in the target test environment, automated checks have passed, and release notes identify the change set. Verified is reserved for QA confirmation using direct retest plus regression impact review; closure cannot occur without evidence attachments.

Severity-based service-level objectives are mandatory. Severity 1 defects include authentication bypass, gateway policy bypass, malware scan skip, unauthorized storage target, or CDN invalidation failure causing stale public identity exposure; these require triage within 30 minutes, engineering response within 1 hour, workaround or fix plan within 4 hours, and validation candidate delivery within 24 hours. Severity 2 defects include incorrect 401 messaging, false accepts or rejects near the file-size boundary, scanner timeout behavior causing visible failure, or UI sync issues that affect some but not all profile surfaces; these require triage within 4 business hours and validated fix delivery within 2 business days. Severity 3 defects include non-blocking usability issues, low-frequency flakiness, or observability gaps; these require triage within 1 business day and target fix delivery within the sprint. Severity 4 defects are cosmetic or documentation-only and are managed through backlog prioritization. Any defect that threatens the 2.0-second SLA or causes residual security ambiguity is automatically elevated one level pending review.

Test management will combine manual exploratory sessions, scripted verification, automation dashboards, and traceability reporting. Every test artifact must be linked to a requirement or architecture control. Manual tests will be stored in Jira/Xray-style structures or equivalent linked test repository with explicit preconditions, datasets, and evidence attachments. Automation results from Playwright, PyTest, JMeter, OWASP ZAP, and integration contracts will publish into the CI pipeline summary and be linked back to Jira through issue keys in commit messages and workflow integrations. Failed automated runs generate either direct defect tickets or investigation tasks depending on confidence and reproducibility.

Peer review and pull-request gating for test scripts are non-optional. Any new or changed automation under ui/, api/, performance/, security/, or integration/ requires one QA peer approval and, for shared framework changes, one additional approval from the test automation maintainer. Pull requests must pass linting, unit checks, secret scanning, and dry-run execution before merge. High-risk suites affecting auth, gateway routing, malware decisions, or CDN synchronization require successful execution in CI on the pull request branch plus green results on the mainline branch before release consumption. No test script may be merged if selectors are unstable, assertions lack requirement mapping, or test data creation is undocumented. This ensures that test assets themselves meet release-quality standards rather than becoming a source of false confidence.

## 4.4 Automation Strategy
Automation will be built as a layered quality architecture rather than a single end-to-end-only harness. Playwright will own browser-based verification of access gating, DOM refresh behavior, multi-session synchronization, upload control visibility, standardized error messaging, and no-full-reload assertions. Playwright network interception will also be used to validate that the browser transmits the raw binary payload to the approved gateway host and does not invoke local transformation endpoints or client-side canvas export paths. For UI tests, authenticated state management will rely on controlled cookie injection fixtures so valid, expired, near-expiry, and missing session scenarios can be replayed deterministically across Chromium-based and at least one secondary browser engine.

PyTest will own API, service-contract, and backend-sequence assertions. The framework will validate gateway authentication outcomes, payload size boundaries, MIME and magic-number enforcement, malware-positive and malware-negative handling, storage path assertions, and event-chain ordering. Contract tests will use correlation IDs to verify that a single upload request maps to a single scanner evaluation, a single persistence decision, and a corresponding purge signal only when allowed. PyTest fixtures will generate test data and manage deterministic environment cleanup in bucket folders so reruns do not inherit stale state.

JMeter will measure latency and throughput across the gateway and end-to-end upload path, especially near the maximum file-size boundary. It will be configured with staged assertions for percentile latency, error rates, and scanner response timing while tcconfig applies 4G network shaping to browser runners and API clients. OWASP ZAP will execute passive and targeted active checks around authorization, request tampering, header trust, and direct-path bypass attempts. tcconfig will be embedded into environment setup jobs to provide reproducible network constraints for SLA validation and to prevent subjective local-network timing results from polluting performance evidence.

Automation repository structure will follow a convention that supports modular ownership and CI isolation: tests/ui/playwright/avatar/, tests/api/pytest/avatar/, tests/integration/avatar/, tests/security/zap/, tests/performance/jmeter/, data/avatar/, fixtures/auth/, fixtures/files/, and .github/workflows/. Shared helper code will live under libs/testutils/ for correlation ID handling, log polling, bucket cleanup, and artifact naming. Each automated script must embed requirement references such as AD-488 and acceptance IDs in metadata tags so reporting can aggregate coverage at release level.

## 4.5 Test Data Management
### Data Requirements
<Identify the type, volume, and data source of test data required, along with masking, scrubbing, and seeding strategies.>

The test data set will include valid JPEG files under 5MB, exact-threshold JPEG and WebP files at 5.00MB, over-limit files at 5.01MB and above, malformed header binaries, renamed text and script files with image extensions, safe malware-flagged samples, and large-dimension but scope-compliant images. Session data will include valid tokens, expired tokens, near-expiry tokens, missing-cookie states, tampered-cookie values, and replay attempts. Data seeding must create isolated user accounts with deterministic avatar reset capability and predefined profile surfaces for synchronization checks. Any production-like personal data is prohibited; synthetic accounts and scrubbed media only are allowed.

## 4.7 Defect Lifecycle, Reporting, and Quality Gates
The defect lifecycle for this initiative remains Open -> Triaged -> In Progress -> Ready for QA -> Verified, with dashboards segmented by severity, root cause category, and release-blocking status. Daily status reporting will include total executed tests, pass/fail counts, blocked cases, mean time to triage, unresolved high-severity defects, and SLA risk trend. A release cannot proceed if any Severity 1 defect is open, any Severity 2 defect affecting acceptance criteria remains unwaived, or traceability coverage for mandatory requirements drops below 100 percent. Waivers require written approval from QA Director, Engineering Lead, Security Lead for security-related findings, and Product Owner.

Quality gates for scripts and code changes are integrated into GitHub pull requests. Branch protections require passing statuses from ui-playwright.yml, api-pytest.yml, integration-avatar.yml, security-zap.yml, and performance-smoke.yml where applicable. Test scripts must follow a branch strategy in which feature branches raise pull requests into INDEXING or the designated integration branch. Required checks include style validation, dependency audit, unit tests for custom helpers, smoke execution of impacted suites, and artifact publication. Peer review must confirm assertion quality, locator resilience, data isolation, and requirement-tag completeness. Any modification to auth fixtures, gateway route configuration mocks, or storage event polling requires secondary approver review because those assets can silently weaken risk coverage.

## 4.9 Tools and CI/CD Integration
The CI/CD integration model is designed so that each test layer provides fast failure signals nearest to the code change while preserving deeper environment-backed assurance before release promotion. GitHub Actions will orchestrate the pipeline. On pull_request events affecting tests/ui/**, src/ui/**, or auth-related configuration, the ui-playwright workflow will run browser-based gating and synchronization tests. On changes affecting API handlers, gateway contracts, validators, or tests/api/**, the api-pytest workflow will execute authentication, boundary, and file-signature suites. On push to INDEXING and on workflow_dispatch for release candidates, the integration-avatar workflow will run the full chain against the integrated environment with log correlation enabled. Performance smoke checks will run nightly and on demand in Pre-Prod; full SLA certification runs will be manually triggered after environment readiness is confirmed.

Playwright’s role in the pipeline is to assert real-browser truths that cannot be safely approximated through API tests alone. These include whether the upload component is visually hidden when it should be, whether the unauthorized block message is rendered consistently, whether dynamic avatar updates occur without page reload, and whether multiple active sessions converge on the same updated avatar after cache purge. Playwright jobs will upload traces, videos, screenshots, console logs, and HAR captures as artifacts so failures can be triaged with exact client-side evidence. The framework will also monitor window.performance navigation entries to ensure no full reload occurs during avatar refresh and inspect outbound requests to verify gateway host usage.

PyTest’s role is to supply precision and breadth at the API and service-contract layers. It will validate HTTP status codes, response bodies, event timing, route exclusivity, storage destination constraints, and malware/validation sequencing at a lower cost than full browser tests. Its parameterization model is ideal for matrixing token states, file sizes, file signatures, and scan outcomes. PyTest jobs will emit JUnit XML, structured logs, and custom JSON traces that can be attached to Jira issues and merged into release dashboards.

JMeter provides statistically meaningful timing analysis and trend comparison across builds. It will be integrated through GitHub Actions runners connected to Pre-Prod or controlled performance environments, executing upload scenarios with files near the 5MB threshold under tcconfig-imposed 4G constraints. SLA reports will capture p50, p95, p99, max latency, error percentage, and component timing annotations from server logs. OWASP ZAP will run in a bounded mode suitable for authenticated upload flows, targeting authorization validation, request tampering, gateway bypass attempts, and header manipulation. tcconfig acts as the reproducibility anchor for network conditions by applying deterministic bandwidth, delay, and packet shaping rules on runners used for browser and API execution.

Representative GitHub Actions triggers and folder conventions are as follows: .github/workflows/ui-playwright.yml triggered on pull_request and push for tests/ui/** and src/frontend/**; .github/workflows/api-pytest.yml triggered on pull_request and push for tests/api/**, src/api/**, and gateway config files; .github/workflows/integration-avatar.yml triggered on push to INDEXING, workflow_dispatch, and scheduled nightly runs; .github/workflows/security-zap.yml triggered weekly, on-demand, and on changes to auth or gateway configuration; .github/workflows/performance-smoke.yml triggered nightly and before release candidate signoff. These workflows publish artifacts to a unified run summary and set mandatory commit statuses used by branch protection.

# 7. Assumptions
<List testing-specific assumptions, including resource availability, team skills, tool access, and upstream data readiness.>

- QA has access to environments with controllable authentication cookies, gateway observability, scanner logs, storage event logs, and CDN purge telemetry.
- Security-approved malware sample surrogates are available for deterministic safe testing.
- Engineering exposes correlation IDs or equivalent request identifiers through logs and events.
- CDN invalidation events are observable within test environments.
- Human-in-the-Loop approvals are available to resume execution after mandated pause checkpoints.

# 9. Risk Mitigation

| Risk ID | Risk Description | Probability | Impact | Mitigation Actions |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| R1 | API Gateway policy bypass allows direct backend upload or alternate host routing | Medium | High | 1) Enforce network ACLs so backend services reject non-gateway origins. 2) Add integration tests that attempt direct service access and confirm denial. 3) Monitor host headers and route telemetry for non-approved ingress paths. 4) Block release if any bypass path succeeds in QA or Pre-Prod. |
| R2 | Malware scanner timeout causes ambiguous acceptance or delayed persistence | Medium | High | 1) Configure fail-closed timeout policy. 2) Instrument scanner start/finish timestamps. 3) Add negative tests for timeout simulation. 4) Implement alerting for timeout rate threshold breaches. |
| R3 | CDN edge cache desynchronization leaves stale avatars visible after successful upload | Medium | High | 1) Validate purge event emission and object-key targeting. 2) Test multi-session and multi-edge freshness checks. 3) Introduce cache-busting telemetry comparison. 4) Prevent release if stale reads exceed tolerance. |
| R4 | Auth token drift or clock skew causes incorrect acceptance/rejection near expiry | Medium | Medium | 1) Define token freshness tolerance explicitly. 2) Execute near-expiry tests with skewed clocks. 3) Centralize time source in environments. 4) Log token-expiry evaluation reason codes. |
| R5 | Browser performs hidden client-side compression or canvas-based mutation | Low | High | 1) Inspect outbound payload hashes and sizes. 2) Disable and detect canvas/export paths in UI code review and tests. 3) Capture HAR evidence in Playwright. 4) Block merge on unapproved front-end processing libraries. |
| R6 | Over-limit uploads are rejected downstream instead of instantly at gateway, wasting resources | Medium | Medium | 1) Measure rejection point with correlation IDs. 2) Add explicit gateway-level assertions for 5.01MB files. 3) Alert on scanner/storage invocation after oversize failures. 4) Review gateway config in every release. |
| R7 | Magic-number validation is incomplete, allowing spoofed files with renamed extensions | Medium | High | 1) Maintain malicious and malformed file corpus. 2) Add contract tests for signature parsing edge cases. 3) Require security review on validator changes. 4) Correlate rejects with validator decision logs. |
| R8 | Storage misconfiguration writes avatars outside authorized cloud ecosystem or bucket path | Low | High | 1) Validate bucket ARN/path in integration tests. 2) Restrict IAM/service account permissions. 3) Monitor write events for region/provider drift. 4) Add deployment guardrails for storage configuration changes. |
| R9 | UI sync logic updates some views but not header bars or active sessions | Medium | Medium | 1) Maintain multi-session Playwright suite. 2) Verify DOM update events across profile and header components. 3) Capture stale element screenshots and logs. 4) Prevent signoff until all surface mappings pass. |
| R10 | Performance budget breach under 4G conditions breaks 2.0-second SLA | Medium | High | 1) Segment latency budgets per component. 2) Run nightly performance smoke and release certification. 3) Use tcconfig for deterministic 4G shaping. 4) Escalate any p95 breach as release blocker until root cause is fixed or formally waived. |

